    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d171">

                <pb facs="0213" n="203" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg203"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.93/6–2747: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-06-27T14:00:00+09:00">June 27, 1947—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 27—4:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1409. Department will have noted series of recent press statements by Dr. Sun
                        Fo<anchor n="80" xml:id="d171-f1"/> with regard to Soviet support of Chinese
                    Communists and China’s need for immediate American assistance; contents <pb
                        facs="0214" n="204" xml:id="pg204"/> of Embtel 1345, June 19, 11 p.m.;
                    Chinese magnification of recent Sinkiang border clash; Chief of Staff’s
                    statements with regard to Soviet-trained Korean military units in Manchuria;
                    Chinese reports to Embassy, not yet confirmed to Embassy’s satisfaction, of
                    removal of Chen Li-fu from office and of declaration of independence of
                    Kwangtung and Kwangsi, also unconfirmed (reEmbtel’s 1364, June 21 and 1406, June
                        26<anchor n="81" xml:id="d171-f2"/>). In same vein, Chen Chi-tien, Minister
                    of Economic Affairs, in interview with Durdin<anchor n="82" xml:id="d171-f3"/>
                    on June 26 stated on record that without outside assistance military and
                    economic collapse is imminent and that Government military units in Shantung and
                    Shansi are already in open revolt.</p>
                <p>These and a variety of other minor occurrences, statements and magnifications
                    within recent weeks lead Embassy to belief that key officials of Chinese
                    Government, recognizing that US has adopted in Europe and Middle East a firm
                    stand against Soviet political expansionism, are constrained to take steps
                    amounting to diplomatic offensive in effort to encourage adoption similar
                    American attitude in China vis-à-vis the Chinese Communists and Soviet
                    Russia.</p>
                <p>It is regrettable, but it is nevertheless a fact, that this constitutes the only
                    discernible current action being undertaken in the face of prevalent and
                    increasing atmosphere of defeatism.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
